§ `Rock Paper Scissors Act`

-- Jason Morris's running Blawx example (Lexpedite/blawx v1.6.22-alpha,
-- blawx/static/blawx/examples/rps.yaml), re-encoded in record style.
-- His ontology: categories game / player / sign; attributes beats (sign -> sign),
-- winner (game -> player), player (game -> player, multi-valued);
-- relationship throw(player, sign, game).
--
-- The section numbers below are the Act's, not the export order's: a decision
-- whose @export/@desc prose opens with a CLEAN section index ("4. ") is pinned
-- to that section, so the emitted attributions read according_to(sec_4_section,
-- ...) and according_to(sec_3_section, ...) and line up with Jason's own
-- workspace names (BLAWX-EXPORT-SPEC R4, spec §11 W3). Two decisions may pin
-- the same section: s.4 is stated once per seat and both land in
-- sec_4_section, whose rule_text is their prose joined in export order.
-- His s.1 ("A game of rock paper scissors has two players") and s.2 (the three
-- signs) are ontology here -- the DECLAREs above -- and so have no decision to
-- pin them; sub-paragraph canvases (his sec_3__para_a_section and friends)
-- remain out of scope, W3(b).
--
-- NLG (BLAWX-EXPORT-SPEC §11 W4): every attribute here carries an `@nlg`,
-- because the synthesised default is the possessive `"has <name> of"` for every
-- object-valued attribute without exception — a verb-shaped default was tried
-- on 2026-09-02 and withdrawn the same day for over-firing on plural nouns
-- (§4.9). `throws` and `beats` therefore write their verb reading out, which is
-- byte-for-byte Jason's own hand NLG for `beats`
-- (`blawx_attribute_nlg(beats,ov,"","beats","")`).
--
-- Placement trap, measured 2026-09-02: on a value-returning `DECIDE … IS` the
-- `@nlg` goes between the app form and the `IS` (as on `beats` below). On the
-- line after `GIVETH` it type-checks and is silently ignored; on the line above
-- `GIVEN` it is a parse error.

DECLARE Sign IS ONE OF Rock, Paper, Scissors

DECLARE Player HAS
    throws IS A Sign  @nlg @(X) throws @(Y)

DECLARE Game HAS
    `first player`  IS A Player  @nlg the first player of @(X) is @(Y)
    `second player` IS A Player  @nlg the second player of @(X) is @(Y)

-- Stated once per seat: L4 records are values and Blawx objects are atoms, so a
-- rule that compares two Player records with EQUALS does not survive the
-- flattening — see the tier-1 finding of 2026-09-02, spec §11 W1.
@export 4. The winner of a game is the player who throws a sign that beats the sign of the other player.
GIVEN g IS A Game
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `first player wins`
@nlg the first player of %g% wins
  IF
  `beats` (g's `first player`'s throws) EQUALS g's `second player`'s throws

-- The other seat. The index alone pins the section; there is no second
-- sentence of s.4 to contribute, so nothing is added to its rule_text.
@export 4.
GIVEN g IS A Game
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `second player wins`
@nlg the second player of %g% wins
  IF
  `beats` (g's `second player`'s throws) EQUALS g's `first player`'s throws

@export 3. The signs are related in the following ways: (a) Rock beats Scissors, (b) Scissors beats Paper, and (c) Paper beats Rock.
GIVEN s IS A Sign
GIVETH A Sign
DECIDE `beats` s
@nlg %s% beats @(Y)
  IS
  CONSIDER s
    WHEN Rock THEN Scissors
    WHEN Scissors THEN Paper
    WHEN Paper THEN Rock

-- Jason's `bobjane` test: jane (first) throws rock, bob (second) throws scissors.
#EVAL `first player wins` OF
        Game WITH
            `first player`  IS Player WITH throws IS Rock
            `second player` IS Player WITH throws IS Scissors
-- L4 oracle ==> TRUE

#EVAL `second player wins` OF
        Game WITH
            `first player`  IS Player WITH throws IS Rock
            `second player` IS Player WITH throws IS Scissors
-- L4 oracle ==> FALSE

-- a tie: nobody wins (in Blawx the winner is simply not derived)
#EVAL `first player wins` OF
        Game WITH
            `first player`  IS Player WITH throws IS Rock
            `second player` IS Player WITH throws IS Rock
-- L4 oracle ==> FALSE

#EVAL `second player wins` OF
        Game WITH
            `first player`  IS Player WITH throws IS Paper
            `second player` IS Player WITH throws IS Scissors
-- L4 oracle ==> TRUE
